-- title:
--   The $t=-1$ specialization of $\rho_3$: kernel generated by the full twist squared $\Delta^4$
-- statement:
--   This is the first step of Birman's proof that the Burau representation of the three-strand braid group is faithful (J. S. Birman, *Braids, Links, and Mapping Class Groups*, Annals of Mathematics Studies 82, Princeton University Press, 1974, Chapter 3, §3.3, Theorem 3.15, pp. 129-130).
--
--   Let $B_3 = \langle \sigma_1, \sigma_2 \mid \sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1 = \sigma_2\sigma_1\sigma_2\rangle$ be Artin's braid group on three strands and let
--
--   $$\rho_3 : B_3 \longrightarrow \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Z}[t,t^{-1}])$$
--
--   be the unreduced Burau representation. Substituting $t = -1$ (the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}[t,t^{-1}] \to \mathbb{Z}$ with $t \mapsto -1$) turns $\rho_3$ into a representation of $B_3$ by integral matrices. The theorem says that this specialised representation has kernel exactly the infinite cyclic subgroup generated by the square of the full twist:
--
--   $$\Delta^4 = (\sigma_1\sigma_2)^6, \qquad \ker\Bigl(\beta \longmapsto \rho_3(\beta)\big|_{t=-1}\Bigr) = \bigl\langle (\sigma_1\sigma_2)^6 \bigr\rangle .$$
--
--   Equivalently, for every braid $\beta \in B_3$ the specialised matrix is the identity if and only if $\beta = (\sigma_1\sigma_2)^{6k}$ for some integer $k$.
--
--   The image of the specialised representation is the homogeneous modular group $M_2 = \mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$; the statement is therefore the classical description of the quotient of $B_3$ by the cyclic subgroup generated by $\Delta^4$. It is the step of Birman's proof in which the Coxeter-Moser presentation of the modular group is used: setting $t=-1$ the Burau matrices of $\sigma_1, \sigma_2$ become the standard generators of $M_2$ with defining relations $s_1s_2s_1 = s_2s_1s_2$ and $(s_1s_2s_1)^4 = 1$, so that the only relators available in this specialised quotient come from the braid relation together with the power relation. It is the reusable input for any argument that pins down the kernel of $\rho_3$ through the modular group.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The evaluation is `LaurentPolynomial.eval₂ (Int.castRingHom ℤ) (-1 : ℤˣ)`, extended entrywise to matrices by `Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.map`; `(6 * k)` is a power in the group $B_3$. The relation $(s_1s_2s_1)^4 = 1$ of the modular group corresponds to $\Delta^4 = (\sigma_1\sigma_2)^6 = (\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1)^4$ in $B_3$, which is why the cyclic subgroup is written as $\langle(\sigma_1\sigma_2)^6\rangle$. `BraidsLinksMCG.sigma ⟨0, _⟩` is $\sigma_1$ and `BraidsLinksMCG.sigma ⟨1, _⟩` is $\sigma_2$.
-- source:
--   J. S. Birman, *Braids, Links, and Mapping Class Groups*, Ann. of Math. Studies 82, Princeton Univ. Press, 1974, Chapter 3 (Magnus representations), §3.3, Theorem 3.15, pp. 129-130 (the step 'setting t = -1 the matrices generate the homogeneous modular group M_2, with defining relations s1s2s1 = s2s1s2 and (s1s2s1)^4 = 1'); cf. Coxeter-Moser 1964, p. 85, and V. Bharathram, J. S. Birman, T. E. Brendle, arXiv:2607.05283v2, Theorem 4.1 (Section 4).

import Definitions.Def_BurauFaithful_UnreducedBurau

set_option autoImplicit false

theorem BurauFaithful.burau_three_spec_kernel (β : BraidsLinksMCG.ArtinBraidGroup 3) :
    Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.map (LaurentPolynomial.eval₂ (Int.castRingHom ℤ) (-1 : ℤˣ))
        (BurauFaithful.burauRep 3 β) = 1 ↔
      ∃ k : ℤ, β = (BraidsLinksMCG.sigma ⟨0, by decide⟩ * BraidsLinksMCG.sigma ⟨1, by decide⟩) ^
        (6 * k) := by sorry
